{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not have dominion over her.</b> The meaning is like 'you shall not make her a lady' (לא תתאמר בה), from the language of lordship (אדנות), in the manner of (Deuteronomy 26): ״את ה' האמרת היום״ — for alef and ayin are interchangeable, and so in the language of the Rabbis: עריס is like אריס. And the sense of the verse is that he may not use her as a maidservant — and see, this is discipline and proper conduct (mussar and derekh eretz) from the Torah." "<b>And it is adjoined</b> to this passage of the beautiful woman (yefat to'ar) the passage ״כי תהיין לאיש שתי נשים האחת אהובה והאחת שנואה״ — to teach you that this wife of beautiful appearance the Torah permitted only with great difficulty, and the Torah spoke only against the evil inclination (yetzer ha-ra), for in the end he hates her, and if he has a son from her, that son will be a rebellious and defiant son (ben sorer u-moreh); therefore it adjoined to it ״כי יהיה לאיש בן סורר ומורה״. And so we find with David, who took Maacah daughter of Talmai king of Geshur when he went out to war — from her issued Absalom, who sought to kill him, and lay with his wives before all Israel, and caused much dissension in Israel, and by his hand thousands and myriads of Israel were slain. And furthermore there was in this measure for measure: he sinned with Bathsheba and was punished through Absalom his son, who was the son of a foreign woman — as it is written (II Samuel 12): ״הנני מקים עליך רעה מביתך״. And adjoined to it further is the passage ״וכי יהיה באיש חטא משפט מות והומת״ — to teach you that if he escapes one trap, he is caught in another. And we learn from the juxtaposition of these passages that transgression draws transgression, for these marriages are not fitting, and even though she converted, this is not a complete conversion, for she did not do so except out of fear and dread of the sword — as even one who comes to convert of his own accord, the Rabbis said they investigate him lest he is converting for money, or for power he might attain, or out of fear, or out of desire; and if he is a man, perhaps he set his eyes upon a Jewish woman, and if she is a woman, perhaps her eyes were set upon one of the young men of Israel. This is the way of our Torah — not like other religions that pursue the nations and seek to expand their faith among them: this one comes to conquer and prevail by the sword against all who refuse to believe, and that one comes to conquer by the force of words and smooth speech and enticements, ויחפאו על ה' דברים אשר לא כן — and this is what Daniel prophesied about them (Daniel 7): ״ופום ממלל רברבן״, and it is written (there): ״ומלין לצד עלאה ימלל וגו'״, (there): ״ויסבר להשניה זמנין ודת״ — that is, he will scheme to change the appointed times and our Torah, but will not succeed; this is the meaning of ויסבר. And it is written further (there 11): ״ועל אל אלים ידבר נפלאות״. And after they investigate him and find no pretext, they inform him of the yoke of the commandments and the toil involved in their performance, in order that he might withdraw. And therefore they did not accept converts in the days of David and Solomon — in the days of David, lest they converted out of fear; in the days of Solomon, lest they converted on account of the kingdom and the bounty that Israel enjoyed. For all who turn from the nations for any of the vanities of the world are not righteous converts (gerei tzedek). Even so, the converts in the days of David and Solomon were many, and the Great Sanhedrin was wary of them: they did not reject them after they had immersed, but neither did they accept them until their end could be seen. And because Solomon converted women and married them, and likewise Samson converted and married — and it is well known that they converted only because of ulterior motives and not by order of a court — Scripture reckoned them as though they are foreign women and stand in their prohibition; and furthermore, their end proved their beginning, for they continued worshipping their idolatry and built for themselves high places (במות), and Scripture attributed it to him as though he built them, as it is said (I Kings 11): ״אז יבנה שלמה במה״. And therefore the Rabbis expounded: ״קשים גרים לישראל כנגע צרעת״ — converts are as hard for Israel as a plague of leprosy, for most of them convert for some ulterior motive and lead Israel astray, and it is difficult to separate from them after they have converted. And everywhere we find that converts were a cause of stumbling and harm for Israel: in the wilderness, in the incident of the Golden Calf, the mixed multitude (erev rav) were the cause; and likewise in the asking for meat (Numbers 11): ״והאספסף אשר בקרבו״ — these are the converts; they were first in all the evil and the beginning of strife. And just as we learned from the juxtaposition of these passages that transgression draws transgression, so too we learn from the passage that commandment draws commandment — for it is written below (Deuteronomy 22): ״כי יקרא קן צפור לפניך״, and what is written after it? ״כי תבנה בית חדש ועשית מעקה״: if you fulfilled the commandment of sending the mother bird, you will merit building a new house and making a parapet; and you will further merit garments of wool and linen — therefore it adjoined to it ״לא תלבש שעטנז״; and you will merit the commandment of tzitzit — therefore it adjoined to it ״גדילים תעשה לך״; and you will merit a wife — therefore it adjoined to it ״כי יקח איש אשה״. And so you have learned from the juxtaposition of passages as well that commandment draws commandment."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:11.488144299Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}